// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_NO_MATCHING_OVERLOAD
//
// Registry 921, form 4 of 4: the OTHER DIRECTION -- too FEW arguments. `@get(i int)`
// called as `b.get()`.
//
// Kept as a fixture of its own rather than folded into the "too many" ones, and the
// row's acceptance demands it in those words. Two reasons, both concrete. A fix that
// compares counts one way only -- say, refusing a surplus while treating a shortfall
// as a defaulted parameter -- would pass every "too many" fixture and close the row
// by carrier. And the two directions fail differently downstream: a surplus reaches
// clang as "too many arguments", while a shortfall reads uninitialised stack, which
// is not a compile error at all.
//
// That second half is why this one matters more than its siblings: the missing
// argument is the case where the old behaviour was not merely a bad diagnostic but a
// silent one.
module neg.p921_arity_method_too_few_neg

type Box priv { n int }
fn Box.new() -> Box => { n: 7 }
fn Box @get(i int) -> int => @n + i

fn main() Io -> () {
    ro b = Box.new()
    // nova:expect E_NO_MATCHING_OVERLOAD -- `get` declares one parameter and the call offers none; the shortfall direction must refuse as loudly as the surplus (#921)
    println(b.get())
}
